<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net9.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <!--
      DELIBERATELY NOT SET: <AssemblyName>.

      Leaving it unset makes the assembly name default to this file's stem, {{MODEL}}, which is
      what keeps the four names aligned. Setting it is the one change that can break the model
      silently: this file would still be called {{MODEL}}.csproj while the DLL came out under a
      different name, and only a web debug (F5) run would notice.

      If you need to change the model's name, run  jcass-dm rename <NewName>  from the Assistant.
      It changes all four names together, or none of them.
    -->
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Framework references come from the sibling refs\ folder, NOT from <ProjectReference>.

    Two reasons this is a wildcard over a folder rather than a list of named assemblies:

      1. You do not have the framework source, so a <ProjectReference> is not an option.
      2. The web Debug Model workspace stages framework DLLs into refs\ for you, and it stages
         far more of them than a local copy does because it includes NuGet transitives. A
         wildcard picks up whatever is actually there, so this exact csproj builds unchanged
         both on your machine and on the debug sidecar. A hard-coded list would need editing
         every time you moved between the two.

    Private=false means "do not copy these into bin\". That is correct and load-bearing: at run
    time the framework host has already loaded its own copy of every one of these assemblies, and
    a duplicate next to your DLL only invites version confusion. Your bin\ folder should contain
    {{MODEL}}.dll and {{MODEL}}.pdb, and nothing else.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Reference Include="refs\*.dll">
      <Private>false</Private>
    </Reference>
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
